sec.p:
(1)
x(t+Δt)=x(t)+dxdt|tΔt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x(t+\Delta t)=x(t)+\left.\frac{d x}{d t}\right|_{t} \Delta t . \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The slope
(2)
slinear=dxdt|t \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s^{\text {linear }}=\left.\frac{d x}{d t}\right|_{t} \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
qualifies as a fitness statistic but often will incorrectly estimate the actual change in relative abundance because x(t) is nonlinear (Fig. 1A, top panel).

Since the relative abundance of a genotype can only be measured at discrete times, we must estimate the instantaneous relative fitness of Eq. (4) using a finite difference between consecutive time points (Fig. S1C, Sec. S4):
(6)
sm≈m(x(t))−m(x(t−Δt)Δt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s^{m} \approx \frac{m(x(t))-m(x(t-\Delta t)}{\Delta t} . \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
For the logit-encoding this becomes
(7)
slogit=1Δt[log⁡x(t)1−x(t)−log⁡x(t−Δt)1−x(t−Δt)]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s^{\operatorname{logit}}=\frac{1}{\Delta t}\left[\log \frac{x(t)}{1-x(t)}-\log \frac{x(t-\Delta t)}{1-x(t-\Delta t)}\right] . \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In the case of a single mutant competing against a wild-type strain, we can rewrite Eq. (7) in terms of the log fold-changes of the mutant and wild-type strains:
(8)
slogit=LFCmut−LFCwtΔt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s^{\operatorname{logit}}=\frac{\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{mut}}-\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{wt}}}{\Delta t}, \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the log fold-change of each strain is LFC = log N (t)/N (t − Δt) and N is the biomass of the strain. The LFC is sometimes also referred to as a Malthusian parameter [9, 26, 65].

Throughout this work, we model the competition between a set of genotypes during a single batch culture growth cycle using the following model of population dynamics [97, 98]:
(12a)
1NjdNjdt=gj(t,R),Nj(0)=xj(0)N0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{1}{N_{j}} \frac{d N_{j}}{d t}=g_{j}(t, R), \quad N_{j}(0)=x_{j}(0) N_{0} \tag{12a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(12b)
dRdt=−∑j1YjdNjdt,R(0)=R0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d R}{d t}=-\sum_{j} \frac{1}{Y_{j}} \frac{d N_{j}}{d t}, \quad R(0)=R_{0} \tag{12b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where for each genotype j, Nj is the absolute abundance, xj(0) the initial relative abundance, gj(t, R) the growth rate, and Yj the biomass yield, where the genotypes include a wild-type as well as one or more mutants. The initial absolute abundance of all genotypes together is N0, and the concentration of the single limiting resource is R with initial value R0. Note that Eq. (12b) assumes that cells consume resources only for biomass growth and not for maintenance of existing biomass.

To capture the sigmoidal shape of typical growth curves with a lag phase, exponential growth phase, and saturation phase (e.g., Fig. S4A), we model the dependence of growth rate on time and resource concentration as
(13)
gj(t,R)=gjΘ(t−λj)Θ(R), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g_{j}(t, R)=g_{j} \Theta\left(t-\lambda_{j}\right) \Theta(R), \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Θ is the step function:
(14)
Θ(z)={0z≤01z>0. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Theta(z)= \begin{cases}0 & z \leq 0 \tag{14}\\ 1 & z>0 .\end{cases}$$\end{document}
In this model, a genotype has an initial lag phase of time εj where no growth occurs, followed by a phase of constant exponential growth at rate gj, and ending when the resource concentration R reaches zero. The time to re-source depletion, which we also call the saturation time, is defined by the implicit equation R(tsat) = 0. The simple form of the growth response (Eq. (13)) means that the absolute abundance of genotype j at saturation is
(15)
Nj(tsat)=Nj(t0)egj(tsat−λj)Θ(tsat−λj) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} N_{j}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}\right)=N_{j}\left(t_{0}\right) e^{g_{j}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}-\lambda_{j}\right) \Theta\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}-\lambda_{j}\right)} \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and its log fold-change is
(16)
LFCj=gj(tsat−λj)Θ(tsat−λj). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{LFC}_{j}=g_{j}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}-\lambda_{j}\right) \Theta\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}-\lambda_{j}\right) . \tag{16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
To calculate relative fitness per-cycle (Eq. (9) and per-generation (Eq. (10)), we numerically determine the saturation time tsat by integrating the biomass and resource dynamics (Eq. (12)) as described in previous work [97, 98]. We note that it also possible to derive an approximate expression for the saturation time tsat which we use for our analytical calculations (Sec. S7).

We define total and pairwise relative fitness,two alternative measures of fitness in bulk competition experiments, as follows. Consider a population with multiple genotypes such that each genotype i has absolute abundance Ni(t) at time t. The total relative abundance of the genotype is
(18)
xi(t)=Ni(t)∑jNj(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{i}(t)=\frac{N_{i}(t)}{\sum_{j} N_{j}(t)} . \tag{18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The “total” here refers to the fact that this is the abundance of genotype i relative to all other genotypes in the population. Similarly, we can think of the definition of relative fitness discussed in the main text (Eq. (4)) as the total relative fitness of this genotype under an encoding m:
(19)
σim=ddtm(xi(t)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{i}^{m}=\frac{d}{d t} m\left(x_{i}(t)\right) . \tag{19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
However, sometimes we want to track the dynamics of a genotype relative to another specific genotype; for example, to follow the fate of a mutant against the wild-type in a bulk competition experiment. For a pair of genotypes i ≠ j, we thus define the pairwise relative abundance
(20)
xij(t)=xi(t)xi(t)+xj(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{i j}(t)=\frac{x_{i}(t)}{x_{i}(t)+x_{j}(t)}, \tag{20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which is the relative abundance of genotype i in a sub-population of only genotypes i and j. To predict the change in the pairwise relative abundance, we can use the slope
(21)
sijm=ddtm(xij(t)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i j}^{m}=\frac{d}{d t} m\left(x_{i j}(t)\right), \tag{21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which defines the pairwise relative fitness of genotype I with respect to genotype j (see Sec. for a generalize definition that covers the case i = j). In the special case of a population with only two strains, the pair-wise relative fitness and total relative fitness are identical (σ2m=s21m)\begin{document}$\left(\sigma_{2}^{m}=s_{21}^{m}\right)$\end{document} but they may differ with more than two genotypes (Sec. S13).

It is also possible to define the total and pairwise relative fitness statistics as finite differences over a time interval, rather than as instantaneous derivatives. For example, we can define them over a growth cycle starting at t = 0 and ending at t = tsat:
(22)
σi,cyclem=m(xi(tsat))−m(xi(0)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{i, \text { cycle }}^{m}=m\left(x_{i}\left(t_{\text {sat }}\right)\right)-m\left(x_{i}(0)\right), \tag{22} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(23)
sij,cyclem=m(xij(tsat))−m(xij(0)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i j, \text { cycle }}^{m}=m\left(x_{i j}\left(t_{\text {sat }}\right)\right)-m\left(x_{i j}(0)\right) . \tag{23} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
For these fitness statistics in the bulk competitions, we use the logit encoding
(24)
σi,cyclelogit=log⁡xi(tsat)1−xi(tsat)−log⁡xi(0)1−xi(0), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{i, \mathrm{cycle}}^{\operatorname{logit}}=\log \frac{x_{i}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}\right)}{1-x_{i}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}\right)}-\log \frac{x_{i}(0)}{1-x_{i}(0)}, \tag{24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(25)
sij,cyclelogit=log⁡xi(tsat)xi(0)−log⁡xj(tsat)xj(0), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i j, \mathrm{cycle}}^{\operatorname{logit}}=\log \frac{x_{i}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}\right)}{x_{i}(0)}-\log \frac{x_{j}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}\right)}{x_{j}(0)}, \tag{25} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where we have rewritten sij,cycle\begin{document}$s_{i j, \text { cycle }}$\end{document} in the form that it is presented in bulk competition experiments [15, 51, 52]. The logit encoding has mathematical advantages for coarse-graining the relative fitness of genotype groups (Sec. S11) but using the log encoding is another common choice in the literature [17, 41, 50]. However, the relative abundances of the individual mutants in our simulated bulk competition experiments are low enough that these two encodings are approximately equivalent (logit x ≈ log x for x ≪ 1).

list-item.p:
Define a group of lineages i = 1, …, k as the reference (e.g., a group of neutral mutants) and sum their total relative abundance
(27)
xref(t)=∑i=1kxi(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{\mathrm{ref}}(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{k} x_{i}(t) . \tag{27} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Calculate the pairwise relative abundance xi,ref of each lineage by
(28)
xi,ref(t)=xi(t)xi(t)+xref(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{i, \mathrm{ref}}(t)=\frac{x_{i}(t)}{x_{i}(t)+x_{\mathrm{ref}}(t)} . \tag{28} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

sec.p:
In this section we explicitly calculate relative fitness of a mutant under a few example models of population dynamics, using the different encodings of relative abundance as described in the main text (main text Fig. 1A). Consider a competition coculture between a wild-type genotype with absolute abundance Nwt(t) and a mutant genotype with absolute abundance Nmut(t). We can describe their dynamics according to the ordinary differential equations (ODEs)
(S1)
1NwtdNwtdt=gwt(t,Nwt,Nmut),1NmutdNmutdt=gmut(t,Nwt,Nmut). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \frac{1}{N_{\mathrm{wt}}} \frac{d N_{\mathrm{wt}}}{d t} & =g_{\mathrm{wt}}\left(t, N_{\mathrm{wt}}, N_{\mathrm{mut}}\right), \\ \frac{1}{N_{\mathrm{mut}}} \frac{d N_{\mathrm{mut}}}{d t} & =g_{\mathrm{mut}}\left(t, N_{\mathrm{wt}}, N_{\mathrm{mut}}\right) . \tag{S1} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Note that the per-capita growth rates gwt and gmut of each genotype can depend on both genotypes to reflect competition or other interactions. The relative abundance of the mutant genotype at time t is
(S2)
x(t)=Nmut(t)Nmut(t)+Nwt(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x(t)=\frac{N_{\mathrm{mut}}(t)}{N_{\mathrm{mut}}(t)+N_{\mathrm{wt}}(t)} . \tag{S2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The dynamics of the mutant relative abundance are therefore described by
(S3)
dxdt=[gmut(t,Nwt,Nmut)−gwt(t,Nwt,Nmut)]×x(1−x). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{array}{r} \frac{d x}{d t}=\left[g_{\mathrm{mut}}\left(t, N_{\mathrm{wt}}, N_{\mathrm{mut}}\right)-g_{\mathrm{wt}}\left(t, N_{\mathrm{wt}}, N_{\mathrm{mut}}\right)\right] \times \\ x(1-x) . \tag{S3} \end{array}$$\end{document}
As defined in the main text (Eq. (4)), the relative fitness of a mutant is sm = dm/dt for an encoding m(x) of the relative abundance x. Under the trivial linear encoding (m(x) = x), the relative fitness is therefore just the right-hand side of the relative abundance ODE (Eq. (S3)):
(S4)
slinear=dxdt=[gmut(t,Nwt,Nmut)−gwt(t,Nwt,Nmut)]×(S4)x(1−x) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} s_{\text {linear }}= & \frac{d x}{d t} \\ = & {\left[g_{\mathrm{mut}}\left(t, N_{\mathrm{wt}}, N_{\mathrm{mut}}\right)-g_{\mathrm{wt}}\left(t, N_{\mathrm{wt}}, N_{\mathrm{mut}}\right)\right] \times_{(\mathrm{S} 4)} } \\ & x(1-x)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
For the log encoding m(x) = log x, we use the identity d log x/dt = x−1dx/dt to obtain the log-encoded relative fitness:
(S5)
slog=ddtlog⁡x=[gmut(t,Nwt,Nmut)−gwt(t,Nwt,Nmut)]×(1−x) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} s^{\log }= & \frac{d}{d t} \log x \\ = & {\left[g_{\mathrm{mut}}\left(t, N_{\mathrm{wt}}, N_{\mathrm{mut}}\right)-g_{\mathrm{wt}}\left(t, N_{\mathrm{wt}}, N_{\mathrm{mut}}\right)\right] \times } \tag{S5}\\ & (1-x) \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Finally, for the logit encoding m(x) = logit x = log(x/(1 − x)), we use the identity d logit x/dt = x−1 (1 − x)−1dx/dt to obtain the logit-encoded relative fitness:
(S6)
slogit=ddtlogitx=gmut(t,Nwt,Nmut)−gwt(t,Nwt,Nmut). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} s^{\operatorname{logit}} & =\frac{d}{d t} \operatorname{logit} x \tag{S6}\\ & =g_{\mathrm{mut}}\left(t, N_{\mathrm{wt}}, N_{\mathrm{mut}}\right)-g_{\mathrm{wt}}\left(t, N_{\mathrm{wt}}, N_{\mathrm{mut}}\right) . \end{align*}$$\end{document}
By comparing relative fitness values under the linear encoding (Eq. (S4)) and under the logit encoding (Eq. (S6)), we see how the logit encoding has removed the explicit dependence on the mutant relative abundance (factors of x and 1 − x), although there can be implicit dependence on the mutant relative abundance within the per-capita growth rates of each strain (gwt and gmut) due to density-dependent growth rates.

Here we give an explicit definition of a genotype’s absolute fitness, analogous to the definition of relative fitness in the main text (Eqs. (1)–(4)). Conceptually, absolute fitness is any number that is sufficient to predict a genotype’s absolute abundance N over a short time window. Let an encoding m(N) be any smooth, strictly-increasing function of the absolute abundance N. We can then predict the absolute abundance over a time window Δt using a linear expansion of the encoded abundance (analogous to main text Eq. (3) for relative fitness):
(S9)
m(N(t+Δt))≈m(N(t))+amΔt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} m(N(t+\Delta t)) \approx m(N(t))+a^{m} \Delta t, \tag{S9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where
(S10)
am=dmdt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} a^{m}=\frac{d m}{d t} \tag{S10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is defined as the absolute fitness of the genotype under the encoding m (analogous to main text Eq. (4) for relative fitness; see also main text Fig. 1B). For example, the absolute fitness of a genotype under the log encoding m(N) = log N is the per-capita growth rate:
(S11)
alog=1NdNdt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} a^{\log }=\frac{1}{N} \frac{d N}{d t} . \tag{S11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In general, the ideal encoding of absolute abundance is the inverse function of the absolute abundance trajectory N (t) (up to a shift and rescaling), so that the first-order expansion in Eq. (S9) is exact and the absolute fitness am is sufficient to determine changes in absolute abundance up to any future time. The log encoding m(N) = log N is therefore ideal when absolute abundance grows or decays exponentially at a constant rate, while the logit encoding m(N) = logit N is ideal for a population that grows with logistic density dependence (Eq. (S7) in case of a single genotype).

Here we show how the logit encoding of relative abundance is related to the logistic model of population dynamics. For a relative abundance x, logistic dynamics are

(S12)

dxdt=rx(1−x),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d x}{d t}=r x(1-x), \tag{S12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



where r is the exponential rate at which relative abundance increases from low values. This form emerges from the general dynamics of relative abundance (Eq. (S3)) when the difference in per-capita growth rates gmut and gwt is constant. Once can also interpret the logistic model as a lowest-order approximation for more complex dynamics. That is, consider a general equation for relative abundance:

(S13)

dxdt=f(x),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d x}{d t}=f(x), \tag{S13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



for an arbitrary function f(x). Since this function must obey the boundary conditions f(0) = 0 and f(1) = 0 (the relative abundance must stop changing when it either goes extinct or fixes), a polynomial expansion of f(x) must have roots at these values:

(S14)

f(x)=rrx(1−x)(1+O(x)).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f(x)=\operatorname{rr} x(1-x)(1+\mathcal{O}(x)) . \tag{S14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Thus the logistic model in Eq. (S12) is a lowest-order approximation even when the true dynamics are more complex.

In a modeling approach typical to many studies in population genetics [118], these population dynamics are captured in the genotype-specific growth factors
(S18)
fmut=Nmut(r+1)Nmut(r),fwt=Nwt(r+1)Nwt(r), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{\mathrm{mut}}=\frac{N_{\mathrm{mut}}(r+1)}{N_{\mathrm{mut}}(r)}, \quad f_{\mathrm{wt}}=\frac{N_{\mathrm{wt}}(r+1)}{N_{\mathrm{wt}}(r)}, \tag{S18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which determine the update equation for the mutant relative abundance
(S19)
x(r+1)=x(r)fmut(1−x(r))fwt+x(r)fmuu, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x(r+1)=\frac{x(r) f_{\mathrm{mut}}}{(1-x(r)) f_{\mathrm{wt}}+x(r) f_{\mathrm{muu}}}, \tag{S19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which is the discrete-time analogue to a differential equation (Eq. (S1)). We divide Eq. (S19) by 1 − x(r + 1) to obtain the form
(S20)
x(r+1)1−x(r+1)=x(r)1−x(r)fmutfwt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{x(r+1)}{1-x(r+1)}=\frac{x(r)}{1-x(r)} \frac{f_{\mathrm{mut}}}{f_{\mathrm{wt}}}, \tag{S20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which allows us to recognize the ratio of growth factors fmut/fwt as a relative fitness statistic, since it is sufficient to predict the relative abundance of the mutant genotype (under the encoding m(x) = x/(1 − x)). But the statistic fmut/fwt acts as a multiplying factor in Eq. (S20), whereas the general form of relative fitness sm (main text Eq. (3)) acts as an additive factor. What is the relationship between the multiplicative and the additive form of discrete-time relative fitness statistics?

To describe discrete rounds of population dynamics in our framework, we treat the relative abundance x(r) as samples from a continuous time series, separated by a time-gap Δt = t(r + 1) − t(r). For a chosen encoding m, The relative abundance of the mutant genotype in the future round is predicted by
(S21)
m(x(r+1))=m(x(r))+smΔt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} m(x(r+1))=m(x(r))+s^{m} \Delta t \tag{S21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
since this is how we defined relative fitness sm in main text Eq. (3). This relative fitness acts as additive factor, but we apply the exponential function on both sides of Eq. (S21) to obtain the updated equation
(S22)
exp⁡[m(x(r+1))]=exp⁡[m(x(r))]⋅exp⁡(smΔt), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \exp [m(x(r+1))]=\exp [m(x(r))] \cdot \exp \left(s^{m} \Delta t\right), \tag{S22} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the additive fitness slogit together with the time scale Δt acts as a multiplicative factor. Specifically for the logit encoding m(x) = logit x we have
(S23)
x(r+1)1−x(r+1)=x(r)1−x(r)exp⁡(slogitΔt). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{x(r+1)}{1-x(r+1)}=\frac{x(r)}{1-x(r)} \exp \left(s^{\operatorname{logit}} \Delta t\right) . \tag{S23} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We compare Eq. (S23) to Eq. (S20) and solve for the relative fitness of the mutant genotype
(S24)
slogit=log⁡fmut−log⁡fwtΔt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s^{\operatorname{logit}}=\frac{\log f_{\mathrm{mut}}-\log f_{\mathrm{wt}}}{\Delta t}, \tag{S24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
as a function of the growth factors fmut, fwt. Using the definition of the growth factors (Eq. (S18)), we see that Eq. (S24) is simply the discrete-time relative fitness for the logit encoding in terms of the mutant and wild-type LFC (main text Eq. (8)). As a general point, we note that the growth-factor fmut qualifies as an absolute fitness (since it is sufficient to predict the absolute abundance N (r +1)), but does not constitute a relative fitness statistic (since we also need to know fwt, see Eq. (S20) or Eq. (S24)).

More generally, for a given encoding function m(x) we define the mutant’s multiplicative relative fitness over a discrete growth cycle as
(S25)
wm=exp⁡(smΔt) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} w^{m}=\exp \left(s^{m} \Delta t\right) \tag{S25} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where sm is the additive relative fitness for this growthcycle under the chosen encoding (main text Eq. (4)) and Δt is the duration of the growthcycle in time units that match sm. Equation (S25) shows that the additive fitness sm has time units, but the multiplicative fitness does not and is formally a dimensionless quantity. These dimensionless units are preserved in the approximate formula
(S26)
wm≈1+smΔt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} w^{m} \approx 1+s^{m} \Delta t, \tag{S26} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which is the first-order expansion of Eq. (S25) in the limit of weak selection (|smΔt| ≪ 1). For example, if we choose to measure relative fitness with the logit encoding on the time scale per-cycle (Δt = 1 cycle), we get
(S27)
wlogit≈1+scyclelogit⋅1cycle \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} w^{\text {logit }} \approx 1+s_{\text {cycle }}^{\text {logit }} \cdot 1 \text { cycle } \tag{S27} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which is the same multiplicative fitness as if we choose to measure the relative fitness on the time-scale per-generation (Δt = LFCwt):
(S28)
wlogit≈1+sgenlogit LFC wt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} w^{\text {logit }} \approx 1+s_{\text {gen }}^{\text {logit }} \text { LFC }^{\mathrm{wt}} . \tag{S28} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In both cases, the time-units for the discrete-time additive fitness cancel in the product term.

To determine how these two fitness statistics lead to different rankings, we consider two competition experiments A and B, which may represent two different mutants competing against the same wild-type or the same mutant tested in two different environments. A mismatch in ranking occurs when the fitness per-cycle in competition is higher in B, while the fitness per-generation is higher in A:
(S32)
scyclelogit(LFCwtB,LFCmutB)>scyclelogit(LFCwtA,LFCmutA),sgenlogit(LFCwtB,LFCmutB)<sgenlogit(LFCwtA,LFCmutA). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & s_{\mathrm{cycle}}^{\operatorname{logit}}\left(\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{wt}}^{B}, \mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{mut}}^{B}\right)>s_{\mathrm{cycle}}^{\operatorname{logit}}\left(\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{wt}}^{A}, \mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{mut}}^{A}\right), \tag{S32}\\ & s_{\mathrm{gen}}^{\operatorname{logit}}\left(\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{wt}}^{B}, \mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{mut}}^{B}\right)<s_{\mathrm{gen}}^{\operatorname{logit}}\left(\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{wt}}^{A}, \mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{mut}}^{A}\right) . \end{align*}$$\end{document}
We insert the expressions for relative fitness per-cycle (Eq. (S30)) and per-generation (Eq. (S31)) into Eq. (S32) to rewrite the condition for ranking mismatch in terms of the LFCs:
(S33a)
LFCmutB>LFCwtB+(LFCmutA−LFCwtA), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{mut}}^{B}>\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{wt}}^{B}+\left(\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{mut}}^{A}-\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{wt}}^{A}\right), \tag{S33a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S33b)
LFCmutB<LFCwtB⋅LFCmutALFCwtA. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{mut}}^{B}<\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{wt}}^{B} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{mut}}^{A}}{\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{wt}}^{A}} . \tag{S33b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
For a given competition A, Eq. (S33) defines an area in the space of LFCs where competition B can lie such that competitions’ fitness is ranked differently per-cycle versus per-generation (main text Fig. 1D shows an example as the red-shaded area). Biologically, these constraints describe a situation where the mutant and wild-type LFCs are both higher or both lower in competition B than in competition A (i.e., so that they grey point is up and to the right of the red point in Fig. 1D, or down and to the left), but the LFC differences must be sufficiently balanced between the mutant and wild-type (i.e., so that the point lies within the red area in Fig. 1D).

Typically, however, the LFCs of the wild-type and the mutant are not independent, since these LFCs are jointly constrained by the fact both strains compete for the same finite resources. For example, assume that a single limiting resource with concentration R is consumed in proportion to the growth of each genotype’s biomass according to
(S34)
dRdt=−1YwtdNwtdt−1YmutdNmutdt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d R}{d t}=-\frac{1}{Y_{\mathrm{wt}}} \frac{d N_{\mathrm{wt}}}{d t}-\frac{1}{Y_{\mathrm{mut}}} \frac{d N_{\mathrm{mut}}}{d t}, \tag{S34} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Ywt and Ymut are the wild-type and mutant biomass yields (stoichiometry of biomass to resource). We can integrate Eq. (S34) to obtain
(S35)
R(t)=R(0)−Nwt(t)−Nwt(0)Ywt−Nmut(t)−Nmut(0)Ymut. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} R(t) & =R(0) \\ - & \frac{N_{\mathrm{wt}}(t)-N_{\mathrm{wt}}(0)}{Y_{\mathrm{wt}}}-\frac{N_{\mathrm{mut}}(t)-N_{\mathrm{mut}}(0)}{Y_{\mathrm{mut}}} . \tag{S35} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
The growth cycle stops when no resource remains (R(t) = 0), such that
(S36)
R(0)+Nwt(0)Ywt+Nmut(0)Ymut=Nwt(0)eLFCwtYwt+Nmut(0)eLFCmutYmut, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} R(0)+\frac{N_{\mathrm{wt}}(0)}{Y_{\mathrm{wt}}}+\frac{N_{\mathrm{mut}}(0)}{Y_{\mathrm{mut}}} & = \\ \frac{N_{\mathrm{wt}}(0) e^{\mathrm{LFC} \mathrm{wt}}}{Y_{\mathrm{wt}}} & +\frac{N_{\mathrm{mut}}(0) e^{\mathrm{LFC}_{\mathrm{mut}}}}{Y_{\mathrm{mut}}}, \tag{S36} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where we have expressed the genotype abundances at the end of the growth cycle in terms of their LFCs. Equation (S36) thus entails a constraint between the wild-type and mutant LFCs. For a set of a mutant competitions with the same initial resource concentration R(0), initial abundances Nwt(0) and Nmut(0), and yields Ywt and Ymut, the mutant and wild-type LFCs are constrained by Eq. (S36) to fall along a one-dimensional curve (black line in Fig. S3A). Geometrically, we see that this constraint on LFCs is incompatible with the requirements for a ranking mismatch between fitness per-cycle and per-generation for a pair of mutant competitions (compare black line and red shaded areas in Fig. S3A). However, if some mutant competitions deviate from this constraint, for example by having different yields Ymut or initial conditions Nwt(0) and Nmut(0), then ranking mismatches may be possible (Fig. S3B).

list-item.p:
The first plateau has a significantly different growth rate that the second one, sucht that the following equation is satisfied
(S37)
gk−1−gk|>0.001+|gk|⋅0.4, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g_{k-1}-g_{k}\left|>0.001+\left|g_{k}\right| \cdot 0.4,\right. \tag{S37} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

sec.p:
In this section, we restate an explicit expression for the saturation time tsat in pairwise competition that was derived in earlier work and allows us to see how mutants can influence resource depletion and the wild-type LFC (main text Eq. (16)). Using the same model of population dynamics (Methods, main text Eq (12)), previous work [123, 124] derived an approximate formula for tsat that shows how it depends on the underlying parameters:
(S38)
tsat≈λ+τ¯LFC, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} t_{\text {sat }} \approx \lambda+\bar{\tau} \mathrm{LFC}, \tag{S38} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where
(S39)
λ¯=τ¯Y¯∑jxjgjλjYj \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \bar{\lambda}=\bar{\tau} \bar{Y} \sum_{j} \frac{x_{j} g_{j} \lambda_{j}}{Y_{j}} \tag{S39} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is the effective lag time of the population,
(S40)
τ¯=[∑jxjgjY¯Yj]−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \bar{\tau}=\left[\sum_{j} x_{j} g_{j} \frac{\bar{Y}}{Y_{j}}\right]^{-1} \tag{S40} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is the effective e-fold growth time (reciprocal exponential growth rate) of the population,
(S41)
Y¯=[∑jxjYj]−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \bar{Y}=\left[\sum_{j} \frac{x_{j}}{Y_{j}}\right]^{-1} \tag{S41} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is the effective yield of the population, and
(S42)
LFC=log⁡(1+R0YN0) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{LFC}=\log \left(1+\frac{R_{0} Y}{N_{0}}\right) \tag{S42} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is the log fold-change of the total biomass, which depends on the initial absolute abundance N0 of all genotypes and the initial concentration of resources R0.

For bulk competition experiments, the saturation time of the culture can be expressed as an interpolation between the traits of the wild-type and the mutant library. We rewrite Eq. (S39) and Eq. (S40) as function of mutant library traits
(S43)
λ¯=τ¯Y¯((1−xlib)gwtλwtYwt+xlibglibλlibYlib), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \bar{\lambda}=\bar{\tau} \bar{Y}\left(\left(1-x_{\mathrm{lib}}\right) \frac{g_{\mathrm{wt}} \lambda_{\mathrm{wt}}}{Y_{\mathrm{wt}}}+x_{\mathrm{lib}} \frac{g_{\mathrm{lib}} \lambda_{\mathrm{lib}}}{Y_{\mathrm{lib}}}\right), \tag{S43} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S44)
τ¯=((1−xlib)gwtY¯Ywt+xlibglibY¯Ylib)−1, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \bar{\tau}=\left(\left(1-x_{\mathrm{lib}}\right) g_{\mathrm{wt}} \frac{\bar{Y}}{Y_{\mathrm{wt}}}+x_{\mathrm{lib}} g_{\mathrm{lib}} \frac{\bar{Y}}{Y_{\mathrm{lib}}}\right)^{-1}, \tag{S44} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S45)
Y¯=(1−xlibYwt+xlibYlib)−1, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \bar{Y}=\left(\frac{1-x_{\mathrm{lib}}}{Y_{\mathrm{wt}}}+\frac{x_{\mathrm{lib}}}{Y_{\mathrm{lib}}}\right)^{-1}, \tag{S45} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where gwt is the wild-type growth rate, λwt is the wildtype lag time,
(S46)
xlib=∑mutsjxj, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{\mathrm{lib}}=\sum_{\mathrm{muts} j} x_{j}, \tag{S46} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is the mutant library abundance at the beginning of the bulk competition,
(S47)
λlib=∑mutsjλjxjxlibgjglibYlibYj \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \lambda_{\mathrm{lib}}=\sum_{\mathrm{muts} j} \lambda_{j} \frac{x_{j}}{x_{\mathrm{lib}}} \frac{g_{j}}{g_{\mathrm{lib}}} \frac{Y_{\mathrm{lib}}}{Y_{j}} \tag{S47} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is the mutant library lag time,
(S48)
glib=∑mutsjgjxjxlibYlibYj \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g_{\mathrm{lib}}=\sum_{\mathrm{muts} j} g_{j} \frac{x_{j}}{x_{\mathrm{lib}}} \frac{Y_{\mathrm{lib}}}{Y_{j}} \tag{S48} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is the mutant library growth rate, and
(S49)
Ylib=[∑mutsjxjxlib1Yj]−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} Y_{\mathrm{lib}}=\left[\sum_{\mathrm{muts} j} \frac{x_{j}}{x_{\mathrm{lib}}} \frac{1}{Y_{j}}\right]^{-1} \tag{S49} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is the mutant library yield. Note that the effective traits in Eq. (S47)–(S49) are independent under a rescaling of the mutant library abundance xlib as long as the relative proportion of the individual lineages xj/xlib stays constant.

To simplify calculations, we sometimes assume that the mutants have variation in lag time and growth rate, but no variation in biomass yield (Yi = Yj for all i and j). Under this assumptions, we can rewrite Eq. (S43) and Eq. (S44) as
(S50)
λ¯=(1−xlib)gwtλwt+xlibglibλlib(1−xlib)gwt+xlibglib, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \bar{\lambda}=\frac{\left(1-x_{\mathrm{lib}}\right) g_{\mathrm{wt}} \lambda_{\mathrm{wt}}+x_{\mathrm{lib}} g_{\mathrm{lib}} \lambda_{\mathrm{lib}}}{\left(1-x_{\mathrm{lib}}\right) g_{\mathrm{wt}}+x_{\mathrm{lib}} g_{\mathrm{lib}}}, \tag{S50} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S51)
τ¯=1(1−xlib)gwt+xlibglib. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \bar{\tau}=\frac{1}{\left(1-x_{\mathrm{lib}}\right) g_{\mathrm{wt}}+x_{\mathrm{lib}} g_{\mathrm{lib}}} . \tag{S51} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

A key result from previous analysis by Wiser et al. [125] on this data set is that the evolving populations increase indefinitely in relative fitness, rather than leveling off at some maximum fitness value. The empirical analysis by Wiser et al. [125] tested the long-term trend by fitting the statistic W=1+sgenlogit\begin{document}$W=1+s_{\text {gen }}^{\text {logit }}$\end{document} to a hyperbolic model of the time series:
(S54)
f(t)=1+att+b, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f(t)=1+\frac{a t}{t+b}, \tag{S54} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where t is the evolutionary time point at which the evolved population is measured against its ancestor, and f (t) is the fitness statistic measured from that competition (here fitted to the measured values of W). The important feature of the hyperbolic model is that it assumes that relative fitness saturates at a maximum fitness over long times (limt⟶∞ f (t) = 1 + a). To contrast this model, they also tested a power law
(S55)
f(t)=(αt+1)β \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f(t)=(\alpha t+1)^{\beta} \tag{S55} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
under which fitness increases without bound over long times (limt⟶∞ f (t) = ∞). To repeat this analysis with the fitness statistics scyclelogit and sgenlogit\begin{document}$s_{\mathrm{gen}}^{\mathrm{logit}}$\end{document} used in this article, we must adjust the models to account for the fact that W takes 1 as its neutral value (occurring at t = 0 by definition) while scyclelogit and sgenlogit\begin{document}$s_{\text {gen }}^{\text {logit }}$\end{document} are zero under neutrality. Thus we use
(S56)
f(t)=att+b \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f(t)=\frac{a t}{t+b} \tag{S56} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
as the hyperbolic model and
(S57)
f(t)=(αt)β \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f(t)=(\alpha t)^{\beta} \tag{S57} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
as the power law model.

Besides AUC it is possible to use other features of the monoculture growth curve as approximate fitness potentials. For example, one can use the monoculture growth rates alone as fitness potentials:
(S60)
s^g=gmutmono−gwtmono, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{s}_{g}=g_{\mathrm{mut}}^{\mathrm{mono}}-g_{\mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{mono}}, \tag{S60} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
or the monoculture lag times:
(S61)
s^lag=λmutmono−λwtmono. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{s}_{\mathrm{lag}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{mut}}^{\mathrm{mono}}-\lambda_{\mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{mono}} . \tag{S61} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Another possible fitness potential is the absolute abundance at saturation, which in our model of population dynamics (Eq. (12)) is proportional to the biomass yield:
(S62)
s^Y=Ymutmono−Ywtmono. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{s}_{Y}=Y_{\mathrm{mut}}^{\mathrm{mono}}-Y_{\mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{mono}} . \tag{S62} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Finally, one can also use the difference in the monoculture log fold-changes (LFCs):
(S63)
s^LFC=log⁡(Nmut(tsatmono)Nmut(0))−log⁡(Nwt(tsatmono)Nwt(0)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{s}_{\mathrm{LFC}}=\log \left(\frac{N_{\mathrm{mut}}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}^{\mathrm{mono}}\right)}{N_{\mathrm{mut}}(0)}\right)-\log \left(\frac{N_{\mathrm{wt}}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}^{\mathrm{mono}}\right)}{N_{\mathrm{wt}}(0)}\right) . \tag{S63} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This looks similar to the definition of relative fitness per-cycle (main text Eq. (9)) but is distinct because it uses the LFCs in monoculture rather than the true LFCs in coculture (which may be different).

In this section, we point out the specific advantages of the logit-encoding for coarse-graining pairwise relative fitness in bulk competition experiments. While the pair-wise relative fitness is defined for any encoding m, the logit encoding endows it with some convenient mathematical properties not shared by other encodings (e.g., the log encoding). The logit encoding of the pairwise relative abundance has the property
(S64)
logitxij=log⁡xi−log⁡xj \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{logit} x_{i j}=\log x_{i}-\log x_{j}$$\end{document}
meaning that it is antisymmetric under exchange of the indices i and j (logit xij = −logit xji) and additive across pairs of indices (logit xij = logit xik + logit xkj). Since the logit-encoded pairwise relative fitness is just the time derivative of the logit function (Eq. (21)), it carries equivalent properties of antisymmetry and additivity:
(S65)
sjilogit=−sijlogit, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{j i}^{\operatorname{logit}}=-s_{i j}^{\operatorname{logit}}, \tag{S65} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S66)
sijlogit=siklogit+skjlogit. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i j}^{\operatorname{logit}}=s_{i k}^{\operatorname{logit}}+s_{k j}^{\operatorname{logit}} . \tag{S66} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We also note that the logit encoding of the pairwise relative abundance has the property:
(S67)
logit(axiaxi+bxj)=logit(xixi+xj)+log⁡(ab). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{logit}\left(\frac{a x_{i}}{a x_{i}+b x_{j}}\right)=\operatorname{logit}\left(\frac{x_{i}}{x_{i}+x_{j}}\right)+\log \left(\frac{a}{b}\right) . \tag{S67} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Rescaling the relative abundances of either genotype thus does not change the pairwise relative fitness (since it only shifts the logit by a constant, which does not affect its derivative). This means that pairwise relative fitness is an “intensive” property of a genotype, analogous to intensive properties in statistical mechanics (such as temperature) that do not scale with system size. For example, if we split a mutant genotype into two subgroups (e.g., differentiated by a neutral marker), the pairwise relative fitness of each mutant subgroup with respect to the wild-type will be the same as the pairwise relative fitness of the mutant genotype as a whole compared to the wild-type. In contrast, the logit-encoded total relative fitness does not satisfy this property since logit(axi) = logit xi+ constant.

When the encoding m is the logit function, the pairwise relative fitness per-cycle still satisfies the above properties (antisymmetry and additivity with respect to indices i, j, and invariance under relative abundance rescaling) since those are properties of the underlying logit encoding. This is also apparent from interpreting the per-cycle fitness statistic as an integral of the instantaneous statistic:
(S68)
sij,cyclem=∫t0tsatsijm(t)dt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i j, \mathrm{cycle}}^{m}=\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{\mathrm{sat}}} s_{i j}^{m}(t) d t . \tag{S68} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

In this section, we generalize the concept of pairwise relative fitness to pairs of genotype groups rather than pairs of individual genotypes. In a multi-genotype population with subsets of genotypes A and ℬ, define
(S69)
xAB(t)=∑i∈Axi(t)∑i∈A∪Bxi(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{\mathcal{A B}}(t)=\frac{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{i}(t)}{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{A} \cup B} x_{i}(t)} . \tag{S69} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
as the relative abundance of genotypes compared to genotypes at time t, where A B is the union of the two index sets. Analogous with main text Eq. (21), we define the fitness of group A relative to group ℬ as
(S70)
sABm=ddtm(xAB(t)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{\mathcal{A} \mathcal{B}}^{m}=\frac{d}{d t} m\left(x_{\mathcal{A} \mathcal{B}}(t)\right) \tag{S70} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
for an encoding m. Under the logit encoding, it turns out that the fitness between these two groups can be conveniently expressed as a weighted average of the pairwise fitness between the member genotypes in each group:
(S71)
sABlogit=1∑i∈Axi1∑j∈Bxj⋅∑i∈A∑j∈Bxixjsijlogit. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{\mathcal{A} \mathcal{B}}^{\text {logit }}=\frac{1}{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{i}} \frac{1}{\sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} x_{j}} \cdot \sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} x_{i} x_{j} s_{i j}^{\text {logit }} . \tag{S71} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
To prove Eq. (S71), we first note that
(S72)
xAB1−xAB=∑i∈Axi∑j∈Bxj. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{x_{\mathcal{A B}}}{1-x_{\mathcal{A B}}}=\frac{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{i}}{\sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} x_{j}} . \tag{S72} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Thus we can rewrite the logit-encoded relative pairwise fitness between A and ℬ as
(S73)
sABlogit=ddtlogitxAB=ddtlog⁡(∑i∈Axi∑j∈Bxj)=ddtlog⁡(∑i∈Axi)−ddtlog⁡(∑j∈Bxj) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} s_{\mathcal{A} \mathcal{B}}^{\operatorname{logit}} & =\frac{d}{d t} \operatorname{logit} x_{\mathcal{A B}} \\ & =\frac{d}{d t} \log \left(\frac{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{i}}{\sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} x_{j}}\right) \tag{S73}\\ & =\frac{d}{d t} \log \left(\sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{i}\right)-\frac{d}{d t} \log \left(\sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} x_{j}\right) \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where we have invoked Eq. (S72) on the second line. We can expand each term on the right-hand side of Eq. (S73) as
(S74)
ddtlog⁡∑i∈Axi=1∑i∈Axi∑i∈Adxidt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d}{d t} \log \sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{i}=\frac{1}{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{i}} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} \frac{d x_{i}}{d t}, \tag{S74} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and then insert Eq. (S74) into Eq. (S73) to obtain
(S75)
sABlogit=1∑i∈Axi1∑j∈Bxj⋅[∑j∈Bxj∑i∈Adxidt−∑i∈Axi∑j∈Bdxjdt], \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} s_{\mathcal{A} \mathcal{B}}^{\operatorname{logit}}= & \frac{1}{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{i}} \frac{1}{\sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} x_{j}} \\ & \cdot\left[\sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} x_{j} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} \frac{d x_{i}}{d t}-\sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{i} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} \frac{d x_{j}}{d t}\right], \tag{S75} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where we have collected the normalization factors (sums over relative abundances in A and ℬ) as a single pre-factor. We then rewrite each product of sums in Eq. (S75) as
(S76)
∑j∈Bxj∑i∈Adxidt=∑j∈B∑i∈Axjxiddtlog⁡xi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} x_{j} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} \frac{d x_{i}}{d t}=\sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{j} x_{i} \frac{d}{d t} \log x_{i} \tag{S76} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
to finally obtain
(S77)
sABlogit=1∑i∈Axi1∑j∈Bxj⋅[∑j∈B∑i∈Axjxi(ddtlog⁡xi−ddtlog⁡xj)]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} s_{\mathcal{A} \mathcal{B}}^{\operatorname{logit}}= & \frac{1}{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{i}} \frac{1}{\sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} x_{j}} \\ & \cdot\left[\sum_{j \in \mathcal{B}} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{A}} x_{j} x_{i}\left(\frac{d}{d t} \log x_{i}-\frac{d}{d t} \log x_{j}\right)\right] . \tag{S77} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Identifying the term in the inner parentheses as the pair-wise selection coefficient sijlogit\begin{document}$s_{i j}^{\operatorname{logit}}$\end{document} (main text Eq. (21)) then results in Eq. (S71).

We finally note that the total relative fitness (instantaneous Eq. (19) and per-cycle Eq. (22) in the main text) is a special case of the relative fitness between groups (Eqs. (S71) and (S79)) where A is the single genotype i and ℬ is all other genotypes besides i:
(S80)
σilogit=11−xi⋅∑j≠ixjsijlogit, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{i}^{\text {logit }}=\frac{1}{1-x_{i}} \cdot \sum_{j \neq i} x_{j} s_{i j}^{\text {logit }}, \tag{S80} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S81)
σi,cyclelogit≈11−xi(0)⋅∑j≠ixj(0)sij,cyclelogit \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{i, \text { cycle }}^{\operatorname{logit}} \approx \frac{1}{1-x_{i}(0)} \cdot \sum_{j \neq i} x_{j}(0) s_{i j, \text { cycle }}^{\text {logit }} \tag{S81} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Consider a bulk competition experiment of a wild-type and a library of large number of mutants over a single batch growth cycle of time tsat (Methods). The total relative fitness of mutant i is (main text Eq. (22))
(S82)
σi=logitxi(tsat)−logitxi(0) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \sigma_{i}=\operatorname{logit} x_{i}\left(t_{\text {sat }}\right)-\operatorname{logit} x_{i}(0)$$\end{document}
while its pairwise relative fitness compared to the wild-type is (main text Eq. (23))
(S83)
si,wt=logitxi,wt(tsat)−logitxi,wt(0) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}=\operatorname{logit} x_{i, \mathrm{wt}}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}\right)-\operatorname{logit} x_{i, \mathrm{wt}}(0)$$\end{document}
Using the coarse-graining rules from Sec. S12 (namely Eqs. (S79) and (S81)), we can express the total relative fitness of mutant i as a weighted sum of the pairwise relative fitnesses between the mutant and the wild-type and between the mutant and the rest of the mutant library:
(S84)
σi≈1xwt+xlib∖i(xwtsi,wt+xlib∖isi,lib∖i), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{i} \approx \frac{1}{x_{\mathrm{wt}}+x_{\mathrm{lib} \backslash i}}\left(x_{\mathrm{wt}} s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}+x_{\mathrm{lib} \backslash i} s_{i, \mathrm{lib} \backslash i}\right), \tag{S84} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the notation lib i refers to the mutant library excluding the mutant i. The approximation here is due to our assumption in Eq. (S79) that selection is weak in enough that the relative abundances of genotypes do not change too much over the growth cycle. Since we can rewrite the pairwise relative fitness between mutant i and the rest of the library as a difference between i and the wild-type and the rest of the library and the wild-type (using Eq. (S66))
(S85)
si,lib∖i=si,wt−slib∖i,wt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i, \mathrm{lib} \backslash i}=s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}-s_{\mathrm{lib} \backslash i, \mathrm{wt}}, \tag{S85} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
we insert this into Eq. (S84) to obtain
(S86)
σi≈si,wt−xlib∖ixwt+xlib∖islib∖i,wt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{i} \approx s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}-\frac{x_{\mathrm{lib} \backslash i}}{x_{\mathrm{wt}}+x_{\mathrm{lib} \backslash i}} s_{\mathrm{lib} \backslash i, \mathrm{wt}} . \tag{S86} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The difference between the total and pairwise relative fitness is therefore
(S87)
σi−si,wt≈−xlib∖ixwt+xlib∖islib∖i,wt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{i}-s_{i, \mathrm{wt}} \approx-\frac{x_{\mathrm{lib} \backslash i}}{x_{\mathrm{wt}}+x_{\mathrm{lib} \backslash i}} s_{\mathrm{lib} \backslash i, \mathrm{wt}} \tag{S87} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Since mutant libraries in these experiments typically contain hundreds or thousands of mutants, the contribution of a single mutant i is small and thus we can assume that the properties of the mutant library excluding mutant i (lib \ i) are approximately the same as the library as a whole (so that xlib\i ≈ xlib, slib\i,wt ≈ slib,wt). This allows us to further simplify the error as
(S88)
σi−si,wt≈−xlibslib,wt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{i}-s_{i, \mathrm{wt}} \approx-x_{\mathrm{lib}} s_{\mathrm{lib}, \mathrm{wt}} . \tag{S88} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Equation (S88) shows why this offset between the total and pairwise relative fitnesses is approximately independent of the focal mutant i (hence the constant shift for all mutant points in main text Fig. 5C). The sign of the error from the frame of reference depends on the mean fitness of the mutant library: a mutant library that is overall deleterious relative to the wild-type (slib,wt < 0) causes the total relative fitness for a mutant σi to over-estimate the mutant’s pairwise relative fitness si,wt. Intuitively, this is because the total relative fitness is comparing the mutant to a mixed population of wild-type and other mutants, which are on average worse competitors than the wild-type, which thus makes the mutant appear to be better than if it is just compared to the wild-type alone (compare top and bottom panel in main text Fig. 5B). Equation (S88) also shows that the error from the frame of reference can be reduced if the mutant library is neutral relative to the wild-type (slib,wt = 0) or the mutant library has small relative abundance in the culture biomass (xlib 1). In bulk competition experiments with barcoded mutant libraries, these assumptions are often not met since the mutant libraries tend to be overall deleterious (as in our simulated bulk competition for the yeast single-gene knockouts, see Fig. S6) and are inoculated at a high relative abundance [146–150] Since this makes the error in Eq. (S88) significant, we instead recommend including barcoded wild-type strains as references in the bulk competition, so that pairwise fitness can be quantified relative to that (Eq. (S83)) rather than using the total relative fitness. By using a mix of bar-coded wild-type cells and non-barcoded wild-type cells it is further possible to optimize this protocol and save on sequencing investment [151].

In the model of population dynamics (main text Methods, Eq. (12)), we can calculate the pairwise relative fitness of genotypes based on the approximation of the saturation time (Sec. S7; [123, 124]). The pairwise relative fitness of genotype i relative to genotype j (per-cycle and under the logit encoding) is
(S89a)
sij,cyclelogit≈sij,lag+sij,growth+sij,coupling, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i j, \text { cycle }}^{\operatorname{logit}} \approx s_{i j, \text { lag }}+s_{i j, \text { growth }}+s_{i j, \text { coupling }}, \tag{S89a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where
(S89b)
sij,lag=−ττiτjΔλij, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i j, \operatorname{lag}}=-\frac{\tau}{\tau_{i} \tau_{j}} \Delta \lambda_{i j}, \tag{S89b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S89c)
sij,growth=−ττiτjΔτijLFC, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i j, \text { growth }}=-\frac{\tau}{\tau_{i} \tau_{j}} \Delta \tau_{i j} \mathrm{LFC}, \tag{S89c} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S89d)
sij,coupling=−τ¯Yτiτj×∑kxkτkYk(ΔτikΔλkj−ΔλikΔτkj) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} s_{i j, \text { coupling }}= & -\frac{\bar{\tau} Y}{\tau_{i} \tau_{j}} \times \tag{S89d}\\ & \sum_{k} \frac{x_{k}}{\tau_{k} Y_{k}}\left(\Delta \tau_{i k} \Delta \lambda_{k j}-\Delta \lambda_{i k} \Delta \tau_{k j}\right) \end{align*}$$\end{document}
The e-fold growth time for genotype j is τj = 1/gj, and the terms Δλij = λi − λj and Δτij = τi − τj are the differences between the two genotypes lag times and growth times. Since the terms in Eq. (S89d) depend on the covariation between growth and lag, we interpret these terms as couplings between the growth and lag phases; they are zero if only two genotypes are present.

In this section, we calculate the error in relative fitness of a mutant arising from higher-order interactions in bulk competition experiments with large mutant libraries, compared to the “true” relative fitness in pair-wise competitions between just the focal mutant and wild-type alone. Here we only consider relative fitness under the logit encoding and measured per growth cycle, so we drop these labels to simplify notation. Let the pairwise relative fitness of mutant i compared to the wild-type (main text Eq. (23)) be
(S90)
si,wtpair=logitxi,wtpair(tsatpair)−logitxi,wt(0) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}}=\operatorname{logit} x_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}^{\mathrm{pair}}\right)-\operatorname{logit} x_{i, \mathrm{wt}}(0) \tag{S90} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
in the pairwise competition with the wild-type alone, and
(S91)
si,wtbulk=logitxi,wtbulk(tsatbulk)−logitxi,wt(0) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}=\operatorname{logit} x_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}\right)-\operatorname{logit} x_{i, \mathrm{wt}}(0) \tag{S91} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
in the bulk competition with all other mutants in the library. The superscripts pair and bulk indicate that the dynamics of xi,wt(t) and the saturation time tsat may be different in the two competitions. The difference between these two measurements of relative fitness is
(S92)
Δsi,wt=si,wtbulk−si,wtpair=si,wtpair(si,wtbulksi,wtpair−1). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \Delta s_{i, \mathrm{wt}} & =s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}-s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}} \\ & =s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}}\left(\frac{s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}}{s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}}}-1\right) . \tag{S92} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Since the difference between the bulk and pairwise competitions is the presence of the other mutants in the library, we interpret this difference as the fitness error from higher-order interactions among the mutants.

We now calculate how this error depends on the underlying growth traits of the genotypes using the population dynamics model (main text Methods, Eq. (12)); and the explicit expression for relative fitness from Sec. S14. Based on the approximate pairwise relative fitness in this model (Eq. (S89)), the relative fitness in the pairwise competition is the sum of two terms
(S93)
si,wtpair=si,wt,lagpair+si,wt,growthpair, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}}=s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \mathrm{lag}}^{\mathrm{pair}}+s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \text { growth }}^{\mathrm{pair}}, \tag{S93} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
while the relative fitness in the bulk competition is the sum of three terms
(S94)
sibulk=si,lagbulk+si,growthbulk+si,couplingbulk, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i}^{\text {bulk }}=s_{i, \text { lag }}^{\text {bulk }}+s_{i, \text { growth }}^{\text {bulk }}+s_{i, \text { coupling }}^{\text {bulk }}, \tag{S94} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the third term represents the coupling between growth and lag phases present only in populations with more than two genotypes (Eq. (S89d)). We define the higher-order effects on the selection for lag time as
(S95)
Δsi,wt,lag=si,wt,lagbulk−si,wt,lagpair. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \mathrm{lag}}=s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \mathrm{lag}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}-s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \mathrm{lag}}^{\mathrm{pair}} . \tag{S95} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Using Eq. (S89b), we can express this in terms of the underlying traits as
(S96)
Δsi,wt,lag=si,wt,lagpair⋅Δτ¯iτ¯pair,i \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \mathrm{lag}}=s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \mathrm{lag}}^{\mathrm{pair}} \cdot \frac{\Delta \bar{\tau}_{i}}{\bar{\tau}^{\mathrm{pair}, i}} \tag{S96} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Δτ¯i=τ¯bulk−τ¯pair,i\begin{document}$\Delta \bar{\tau}_{i}=\bar{\tau}^{\text {bulk }}-\bar{\tau}^{\text {pair }, i}$\end{document} is the difference in effective e-fold growth times (Eq. (S40)) in the bulk competition and in the pairwise competition of mutant i and the wild-type.

We similarly define the higher-order effects in the selection on growth rate as
(S97)
Δsi,wt,growth=si,wt,growthbulk−si,wt,growthpair, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta s_{i, \text { wt }, \text { growth }}=s_{i, \text { wt }, \text { growth }}^{\text {bulk }}-s_{i, \text { wt }, \text { growth }}^{\text {pair }}, \tag{S97} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and calculate si,wt,growthbulk\begin{document}$s_{i, \text { wt }, \text { growth }}^{\text {bulk }}$\end{document} and si,wt,growthpair\begin{document}$s_{i, \text { wt }, \text { growth }}^{\text {pair }}$\end{document} from Eq. (S89c) to get the expression
(S98)
Δsi,wt,growth=−Δτi,wtτwtτi(τ¯bulkLFCbulk−τ¯pair,iLFCpair,i) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & \Delta s_{i, \text { wt,growth }}= \\ & \quad-\frac{\Delta \tau_{i, \mathrm{wt}}}{\tau_{\mathrm{wt}} \tau_{i}}\left(\bar{\tau}^{\text {bulk }} \mathrm{LFC}^{\text {bulk }}-\bar{\tau}^{\text {pair }, i} \mathrm{LFC}^{\text {pair }, i}\right) \tag{S98} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where LFCbulk is the log fold-change (Eq. (S42)) of the total biomass in the bulk competition and LFCpair,i is the log fold-change of the total biomass in the pairwise competition between mutant i and the wild-type. We note that total biomass growth in the bulk competition LFCbulk depends on the mutant library through the effective biomass yield (Eq. (S41)), but this dependence is weak because the yield enters only logarithmically into Eq. (S42). We thus assume that the bulk and pair-wise competition have equal biomass growth (LFCbulk LFCpair,i) for all mutants i such that the higher-order effect on the growth rate selection (Eq. (S98)) is proportional to the increase in the mean doubling time
(S99)
Δsi,wt,growth≈si,wt,growthpairΔτ¯iτ¯―pair,i. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta s_{i, \text { wt }, \text { growth }} \approx s_{i, \text { wt }, \text { growth }}^{\text {pair }} \frac{\Delta \bar{\tau}_{i}}{\overline{\bar{\tau}} \text { pair }, i} . \tag{S99} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Since this has the same form as the higher-order effect on lag time selection in Eq. (S96), we can combine Eq. (S99) and Eq. (S96)) into
(S100)
Δsi,wt,lag+Δsi,wt,growth≈si,wtpairΔτ¯iτ¯pair,i. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \mathrm{lag}}+\Delta s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \text { growth }} \approx s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}} \frac{\Delta \bar{\tau}_{i}}{\bar{\tau} \text { pair }, i} . \tag{S100} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Because the effective growth time in the pairwise competition τ¯pair,i\begin{document}$\bar{\tau}^{\text {pair }, i}$\end{document} is approximately just the effective growth time of the wild-type alone τwt (assuming the mutant is competed against it at low initial relative abundance), this means that
(S101)
Δsi,wt,lag+Δsi,wt,growthsi,wtpair≈(τ¯bulkτwt−1). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\Delta s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \mathrm{lag}}+\Delta s_{i, \mathrm{wt}, \text { growth }}}{s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}}} \approx\left(\frac{\bar{\tau}^{\text {bulk }}}{\tau_{\mathrm{wt}}}-1\right) . \tag{S101} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
That is, the relative error of lag and growth selection from higher-order interactions is approximately independent of the individual mutant, but rather depends on properties of the wild-type (τwt) and the whole mutant library (through τ¯bulk\begin{document}$\bar{\tau}^{\text {bulk }}$\end{document}). This is why we observe an approximately constant slope across all mutants in Fig. (dark orange points) when comparing the error against the pairwise relative fitness. The slight deviation from the constant slope is due to our approximation that the LFCs are the same between the bulk and pairwise competitions; this is a good approximation for the yeast deletion library but not exactly true, and hence causes a slightly different scaling between the growth Δsgrowth and lag Δslag error terms compared to Eq. (S101).

We start by pointing out that we can express the relative fitness in pairwise (Eq. (S90)) and bulk competitions (Eq. (S91)) in terms of the saturation times for these competitions, using the explicit solution to the population dynamics model in main text Eq. (15):
(S103)
si,wtpair=gi(tsatpair,i−λi)−gwt(tsatpair,i−λwt), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}}=g_{i}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}^{\mathrm{pair}, i}-\lambda_{i}\right)-g_{\mathrm{wt}}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}^{\mathrm{pair}, i}-\lambda_{\mathrm{wt}}\right), \tag{S103} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S104)
si,wtbulk=gi(tsatbulk−λi)−gwt(tsatbulk−λwt). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}=g_{i}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}-\lambda_{i}\right)-g_{\mathrm{wt}}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}-\lambda_{\mathrm{wt}}\right) . \tag{S104} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We can thus express the error from higher-order interactions (Eq. (S92)) as
(S105)
Δsi,wt=Δgi,wt(tsatbulk−tsatpair,i), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta s_{i, \mathrm{wt}}=\Delta g_{i, \mathrm{wt}}\left(t_{\mathrm{sat}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}-t_{\mathrm{sat}}^{\mathrm{pair}, i}\right), \tag{S105} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Δgi,wt = gi − gwt. Equation (S105) shows that the mutant library affects the fitness of individual mutants siwtbulk\begin{document}$s_{i \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{bulk}}$\end{document} by changing the saturation time. Mathematically, this is equivalent to the results of Sec. S15, which showed how the difference in effective e-fold growth times τ\begin{document}$\tau$\end{document} between bulk and pairwise competitions primarily mediated the higher-order effects, but expressed in terms of the saturation time tsat (which is not identical to τ\begin{document}$\tau$\end{document} but related through Eq. (S38)). The error in Eq. (S105) is also proportional to the growth rate advantage of the mutant compared to the wild-type. In particular, mutant genotypes that only differ in lag time are not affected by the mutant library since their advantage is accrued once at the beginning of the growth cycle and therefore does not scale with the total time of competition.

We thus next consider the case where ϵAi < 1. We multiply both sides of Eq. (S111) by the denominator on the left-hand side (always positive) to obtain
(S113)
xlibglib<ϵAi(xlibglib+(1−xlib)gwt), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{\mathrm{lib}} g_{\mathrm{lib}}<\epsilon A_{i}\left(x_{\mathrm{lib}} g_{\mathrm{lib}}+\left(1-x_{\mathrm{lib}}\right) g_{\mathrm{wt}}\right), \tag{S113} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and then collect all the terms involving xlib on the left hand side:
(S114)
xlib((1−ϵAi)glib+ϵAigwt)<ϵAigwt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{\mathrm{lib}}\left(\left(1-\epsilon A_{i}\right) g_{\mathrm{lib}}+\epsilon A_{i} g_{\mathrm{wt}}\right)<\epsilon A_{i} g_{\mathrm{wt}} . \tag{S114} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Since ϵAi < 1, the factor multiplying xlib on the left-hand side of Eq. (S114) must be positive and thus we can divide both sides to obtain an upper bound on the library abundance xlib such that the relative error on fitness is less than ϵ:
(S115)
xlib<ϵAigwt(1−ϵAi)glib+ϵAigwt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{\mathrm{lib}}<\frac{\epsilon A_{i} g_{\mathrm{wt}}}{\left(1-\epsilon A_{i}\right) g_{\mathrm{lib}}+\epsilon A_{i} g_{\mathrm{wt}}} . \tag{S115} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Since ϵ will typically be small, we can simplify the right-hand side of Eq. (S115) by approximating it to first-order in ϵ:
(S116)
xlib⪅ϵAigwtglib. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{\operatorname{lib}} \lessapprox \epsilon A_{i} \frac{g_{\mathrm{wt}}}{g_{\mathrm{lib}}} . \tag{S116} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This approximation holds as long as ϵAi(glib − gwt)/glib ≪1.

The maximum mutant library abundance determined by Eq. (S115) or (S116) is specific to a single mutant genotype i, meaning that the relative fitness errors for other mutants may exceed ϵ even if the inequality for mutant i is satisfied. To keep the relative fitness errors for all mutants less than ϵ, we need to choose a library abundance xlib∗\begin{document}$x_{\mathrm{lib}}^{*}$\end{document} such that
(S117)
maxmutsj|Δsj,wtsj,wtpair|<ϵ. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \max _{\text {muts } j}\left|\frac{\Delta s_{j, \mathrm{wt}}}{s_{j, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}}}\right|<\epsilon . \tag{S117} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This means that Eq. (S116) must be satisfied for all mutants j:
(S118)
xlib∗=minmutsjϵAjgwtglib. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{\mathrm{lib}}^{*}=\min _{\mathrm{muts} j} \epsilon A_{j} \frac{g_{\mathrm{wt}}}{g_{\mathrm{lib}}} . \tag{S118} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Thus we must determine the minimum value of Aj over all mutants j. Using the definition in Eq. (S112), Aj is minimized for the mutant with minimum vale of
(S119)
sj,wtparrΔgj,wt|=|1gwtLFC−gjΔλj,wtΔgj,wt|, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{s_{j, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{parr}}}{\Delta g_{j, \mathrm{wt}}}\left|=\left|\frac{1}{g_{\mathrm{wt}}} \mathrm{LFC}-g_{j} \frac{\Delta \lambda_{j, \mathrm{wt}}}{\Delta g_{j, \mathrm{wt}}}\right|,\right. \tag{S119} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where we have calculated the pairwise relative fitness in Eq. (S103) using tsatpair,i\begin{document}$t_{\text {sat }}^{\text {pair }, i}$\end{document} from Eq. (S106). Mutants that minimize Eq. (S119) are those with tradeoffs between lag times and growth rates such that their overall pair-wise relative fitness with respect to the wild-type is zero. This makes sense, since these mutants will have a relative fitness close to zero in the pairwise competition (|sj,wtpair|≪1)\begin{document}$\left(\left|s_{j, \mathrm{wt}}^{\mathrm{pair}}\right| \ll 1\right)$\end{document}, but nonzero fitness relative to the wild-type in the bulk competition as the relative selection on the lag time and growth rate shifts (e.g., because τ¯bulk>τ¯pair\begin{document}$\bar{\tau}^{\text {bulk }}>\bar{\tau}^{\text {pair }}$\end{document} in Eq. (S89)).
